# Pull requests

This repository is operated by an agent, not by hand. The issue, the diff and the pull
request body are the whole record of a change, so each one is written for a reader who was
not in the conversation.

## From an issue

1. Read the issue as **Why / What / Where / Who / When / How**. When a section is missing and the answer
   would change the work, ask in the issue first; otherwise state the assumption in the pull
   request body.
2. Reproduce before fixing: a failing unit-test row or fixture case that shows the bug or
   the missing behaviour.
3. Branch from `main`, named `type/topic` (`fix/valibot-enum`, `feat/x-brand`). Never commit
   to `main` — a version bump there publishes to npm.
4. One topic per pull request. A generator fix and a docs rewrite are two pull requests.

## The title is the changelog

`.github/workflows/release.yml` cuts each GitHub Release with
`gh release create --generate-notes`, and those notes are the **titles of the pull
requests merged since the previous release**. Write the title for someone reading the
releases page, not for the diff:

```text
type(scope): summary in the imperative, no trailing period

type   feat | fix | perf | refactor | docs | test | build | ci | chore
scope  zod | valibot | effect | typebox | arktype | ajv | yup | cli | parser | fixtures | docs | ci
```

```text
fix(valibot): emit valid schemas for null and object enum members
feat(zod): support x-stringbool on boolean properties
docs: point Effect Schema links at the v4 documentation
```

## Body

Fill in `.github/pull_request_template.md`. Keep all six headings, replace each comment
with the answer, and write `None.` under a heading that does not apply rather than
deleting it. One to three sentences per heading.

| Heading | Answer                                                                       |
| ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Why     | the bug, gap or request; `Closes #123`                                       |
| What    | the change as a user of the package sees it                                  |
| Where   | generators, parser, CLI, fixtures or docs touched — and what was left out    |
| Who     | who notices: every user, one target's users, contributors; breaking for whom |
| When    | release impact: none, next release, or the version bumped in this change     |
| How     | the approach, then the evidence: the gate below, and the fixture diff        |

The evidence that matters most here is the **fixture diff**: this repository's review
artifact is `fixtures/*/*/output.ts`, so paste the interesting part of it or say plainly
that generated output is unchanged.

## Labels

Apply exactly one: `bug`, `enhancement`, `documentation`, `breaking` or `dependencies`.
`.github/release.yml` groups the generated notes by that label, and anything unlabelled
falls into "Other changes".

## Green before you open it

```bash
vp run -r check       # format, lint, types
vp run lint           # Markdown, terminology, spelling, secrets, links
vp run schema-truth#build && vp run -r typecheck   # generated code against the real libraries
vp test && vp run schema-truth#test
```

Tick a box in the template only for a command that ran and passed. A command that failed
is pasted, not hidden, and a pull request is not opened over a red gate.

## A version bump is a release

`release.yml` tags `v<version>`, publishes to npm and creates the GitHub release as soon
as `main` carries a version of `packages/schema-truth/package.json` that is not on
the registry. Bumping that version **is** the release — never include a bump in a pull
request that was not asked to publish one.

## Opening it

```bash
gh pr create --base main --title 'fix(zod): …' --label bug --body-file <file>
```

Where `gh` is not available, open the pull request through the platform's own step with
the same title, label and body.
